WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:08.480
ChatGPT today has incredible capabilities. It is&nbsp;
like an engineer who helps you write a video game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:12.520
it is like a scholar who helps you write a paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:17.400
and it is like an assistant who&nbsp;
organizes your life, and files.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.680
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:29.160
Today, large language model assistants understand&nbsp;
text and they understand images. They have for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:34.680
quite a while now. However, connecting&nbsp;
the two still remains a challenge. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:40.680
with GPT-4’s vision model, we can dump&nbsp;
all this information into a robot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:48.560
and it will be able to take commands from us, and&nbsp;
make a plan and perform it. And it gets better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:55.680
it promises that it also has some common sense.&nbsp;
Something that is not common at all in today’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:02.800
world. So can it deliver these promises?&nbsp;
Let’s see together through 3 experiments.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:10.480
Experiment 1. Here, we would like to get some&nbsp;
coke, but, look. Haha! There is no coke anywhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:18.440
to be seen. Now’s your time to shine, little AI,&nbsp;
and then, nice. It opens the fridge very gently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:26.680
gets the coke, closes the fridge, and&nbsp;
there we go. Excellent. It can also do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:33.160
similar other gentle tasks, such as get&nbsp;
some pringles and pour it onto a plate.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:41.720
Experiment 2. Pick up the marvel model. It is,&nbsp;
of course, behind a strategically placed glass.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:48.800
And without additional instructions, it puts&nbsp;
it away safely, and now we can play. Great!

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:56.880
Now experiment 3. This is a bit harder perhaps.&nbsp;
Organize the sushi pieces to be like they are in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:04.880
this image. A tricky, multi-modal question.&nbsp;
Can it do it? Oh yes, well done little AI!

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:08.520
Now two good news and one bad news.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:13.800
Good news number one, yes! This one&nbsp;
is also doing one of my favorites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.960
and that is, learning safely&nbsp;
in a computer simulation first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:24.760
and only when it demonstrated that it is smart&nbsp;
and safe, can it move around in the real world.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:31.560
Good news number two - this can perform all of&nbsp;
these tasks in a zero-shot manner. This means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:38.200
that it can perform a broad spectrum of these&nbsp;
everyday tasks without being specifically trained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:44.640
for it. What does that mean exactly? Well, if&nbsp;
you give it a task that it hasn’t seen before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:50.720
it can lean on its knowledge of the world a&nbsp;
bit like a human would and have a good chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:57.840
of still being able to do it. If you ask me,&nbsp;
that is one possible definition of intelligence.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.880
Now, less good news - not even this&nbsp;
technique is perfect. Look. Chain of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:08.920
thought. This means that with the prompts, it&nbsp;
gets a little help. At the risk of simplifying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:14.080
this help comes in the form of a few&nbsp;
exemplars that outlines the reasoning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:19.480
process for a few tasks for the AI&nbsp;
to lean on. It’s not that bad. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:24.920
I bet that just one more paper down the&nbsp;
line, and this might not even be needed.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:32.120
But today, ChatGPT can do so much more. For&nbsp;
instance, here it helps this Fellow Scholar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:39.000
write a video game without the user having to&nbsp;
write a single line of code. First, we need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:46.400
briefly describe the game. And it gets to work,&nbsp;
and then, the first version is already playable!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:54.560
Excellent! Wait, the missiles are not exploding,&nbsp;
so, is the dream over? Of course not! Look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.560 --> 00:04:01.920
after taking a couple screenshots of the game,&nbsp;
don’t forget, GPT is multimodal, it can look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:08.400
images, and then, we can explain the issues,&nbsp;
we need some explosions, collision detection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:16.920
and. Look at that! A significantly better game in&nbsp;
a matter of seconds. Explosions are now happening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:24.600
now add a scoreboard to the game, a start screen,&nbsp;
and a damage/health system. It explains what it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:31.320
is about to do, and there we go. A proper&nbsp;
little arcade game. This is like having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:37.920
a super fast, super smart engineer at your&nbsp;
fingertips. This is absolutely incredible.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:43.880
But this is not only an engineer, but&nbsp;
it can explain mathematical jokes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:49.120
This is one of my favorites. This one is&nbsp;
about explaining the difference between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:56.640
discrete and continuous mathematics. Love it.
But wait, it’s not only good for explaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:04.440
mathematical jokes, but also for doing mathematics&nbsp;
and writing papers. Take this from one of the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:12.400
mathematicians in the world today, Terence Tao.&nbsp;
Even he is using GPT-4 too and he says as follows:

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:20.240
“I could feed GPT-4 the first few PDF pages of&nbsp;
a recent math preprint and get it to generate a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:26.240
half-dozen intelligent questions that an expert&nbsp;
attending a talk on the preprint could ask.”

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.680
“I plan to use variants of such prompts to prepare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:36.600
my future presentations or to begin&nbsp;
reading a technically complex paper.”

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:42.360
Whoa! One of the finest mathematicians&nbsp;
of our generation is asking it for help,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:48.960
and gets meaningful questions and sometimes&nbsp;
answers. But…wait a second. Does that not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:53.640
sound like a helpful colleague&nbsp;
or a co-author to you? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:58.000
hold on to your papers Fellow Scholars,&nbsp;
because here comes the bombshell:&nbsp;

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:03.800
“When integrated with tools such as&nbsp;
formal proof verifiers, internet search,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:10.840
and symbolic math packages, I expect,&nbsp;
say, 2026-level AI, when used properly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:18.080
will be a trustworthy co-author in mathematical&nbsp;
research, and in many other fields as well.”

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:18.840
Wow.

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:25.680
And we are not done yet, not even close! I&nbsp;
have one more amazing application for you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:32.480
It can also take a bunch of your files, and take&nbsp;
some work off of your shoulders by once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:38.400
using common sense, and chucking them into&nbsp;
the appropriate directories. The clutter is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:47.080
now gone. Now of course, this workflow is not&nbsp;
as convenient as it could be. Yet. But just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:53.280
imagine if this kind of useful tool could be&nbsp;
integrated deeper into the operating system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:58.760
and not have to work outside of it. That&nbsp;
would help us spend less and less time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:06.320
on organizing things, and create or learn&nbsp;
something new instead. Love it. So good!

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:11.960
Fellow Scholars, what you are seeing here&nbsp;
is perhaps the birth of a new tool that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:19.720
might become more useful than any other tool I&nbsp;
have ever seen. Wow. What a time to be alive!

